WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, you have sold
tens of millions of books,

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:02.602 line:92%
but I did not realize this --

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.738 align:start size:94% position:6%
that when "The Kite Runner"
came out,

00:00:04.738 --> 00:00:07.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
you were still working
as an internist.

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:07.907 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-I was, indeed.

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So you would have patients
come and see you.

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:10.610 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Were they familiar?

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.212
Did they know at this point
that you had written that book?

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:14.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, they were finding out,
then, eventually,

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:17.517
it became a health hazard
because I had 20 minutes

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:20.954
to manage their heart failure,
their thyroid, their diabetes,

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:22.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I was spending
like 15 minutes of it

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:25.091 size:84% position:42%
discussing plot points
of "The Kite Runner"...

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:25.825 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Light laughter ]

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:27.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
...and signing books
for Uncle Joe.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:28.962 line:92%
And after a while, I was like,

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:30.530 align:start size:94% position:6%
"This is not good
for you, for sure."

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:31.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's probably not good for me,
either,"

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:33.867
So then I was like, "Yeah,
probably should step away."

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.202 size:78% position:39%
-So you stepped away
and made the decision

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:37.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be a full-time novelist.
-Yes.

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:39.406 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Something that is obviously
a little easier to do

00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:42.642
when you are already the author
of a very successful book.

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:43.610 line:92%
How did your family react?

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.646
How were they when you told them
about this career change?

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:48.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Skeptical.
-Uh-huh.

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:49.849 line:92%
Even after "The Kite Runner"?

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:52.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, well after
"The Kite Runner."

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.421 align:start size:94% position:6%
When I told my parents
that I was thinking

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:55.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
about stepping away
from medicine

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:58.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
and writing full time,
my mom was --

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:00.260 align:start size:94% position:6%
diplomatically, she goes,
"Are you sure?"

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
I said,
"Yeah, I think we're okay."

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:03.530 align:start size:94% position:6%
She said,
"You know, I went to Costco,

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.498 size:75% position:38%
and they didn't have
'The Kite Runner.'"

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:06.966 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
"So you may not be --

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:09.969 size:81% position:41%
may not be as popular
as you think you are."

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:10.737 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:14.107
And thankfully, when I published
"A Thousand Splendid Suns,"

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
she walked into the room
with a copy,

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:17.844 align:start size:94% position:6%
and she goes,
"Guess where I got this."

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:18.645 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:21.214 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was like,
"Whoo-hoo! I've arrived."

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:23.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Applause ]
-That is wonderful.

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:25.685 size:81% position:41% line:92%
[ Applause continues ]

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:26.753 size:88% position:44% line:92%
So, this is obviously --

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:30.457 align:start size:94% position:6%
this book covers a very sad,
a very serious topic.

00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:33.159 align:start size:94% position:6%
This was inspired
by the photo of Alan Kurdi,

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:36.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
who was a young boy
who was trying to escape Syria

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.597 size:75% position:38% line:92%
and washed ashore --

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:38.598 line:92%
his dead body washed ashore.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:39.966 align:start size:94% position:6%
That was the photo
that inspired this book.

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:42.202
What was it about seeing
that that made you think,

00:01:42.202 --> 00:01:44.170 align:start size:94% position:6%
"This is something
I want to explore,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.271 align:start size:94% position:6%
something I want
to write about"?

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.307 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, look, I'm a dad.
I have two kids.

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:50.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
When I saw that photograph,
I was just bludgeoned,

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:52.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
like millions of people
around the world.

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:54.180 size:78% position:39%
And part of the power
of that photograph

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.116 line:92%
is that Alan is lying facedown,

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:57.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you can't
quite see his face.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.253
So it's very easy to project
the face of your own children,

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:03.656
the people that you love,
upon -- on that photograph.

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.825 line:92%
And so, I wrote "Sea Prayer"

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:08.194 align:start size:94% position:6%
to not only help keep
his memory alive

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.965
but, also, to remind all of us
the moral outrage we all felt,

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:15.301
the indignation
we felt collectively, globally,

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:16.236 line:92%
when we saw that photograph,

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:19.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
which was such a brutal
and poignant symbol

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:20.440 line:92%
of not only the Syrian War,

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.076
but the plight of millions
of refugees around the world.

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:25.845
And so, this is meant to be
a tribute not only to him,

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:27.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
but to, also,
the thousands of families

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:30.250
who've been cornered into making
that same kind of decision.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.851 size:88% position:44%
-And I should say all of
the money from this book

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:33.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
is going to
the UN Refugee Agency.

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:35.088 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-We were talking backstage.

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:36.022 line:92%
You were born in Afghanistan.

00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:38.458 align:start size:94% position:6%
You came to this country
38 years ago today.

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:41.227 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Today, yeah.
-And your family...

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.296 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:46.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
You settled in San Jose, yes?
-Yes, yeah.

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:48.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, one of the things
that get lost is,

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:50.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
I feel like,
when we hear "refugee,"

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:53.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
all we know
about their biography

00:02:53.473 --> 00:02:57.076 align:start size:94% position:6%
is they escaped there
to come here,

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:58.578 line:92%
and we lose a lot of their --

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:01.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
the individuality that,
obviously, every refugee has.

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:04.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
How -- How does it --
How do you feel

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:05.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the way
we talk about refugees,

00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:07.287 align:start size:94% position:6%
and was that the case
when your family came here?

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:11.057
Did you lose a part of you that
you left behind in Afghanistan?

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:12.959 size:78% position:39%
-Well, the experience
of being a refugee

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:14.093 line:92%
is -- is certainly a loss

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:16.262 align:start size:94% position:6%
of your land, your community,
your home,

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.131 align:start size:94% position:6%
all the things
you're familiar with.

00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:20.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
But we tend to talk, I think,
about refugees

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:21.301 size:84% position:42% line:92%
in very unhelpful ways,

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:24.070
as sort of this monolithic
community of needy people.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
The fact is that refugees
in this country

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:29.742 size:81% position:41%
are essential strands
of the American story.

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:34.314
You know, America has a very
long history with refugees.

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:36.216 align:start size:94% position:6%
In this country,
refugees are teachers.

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:38.117 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're advocates.
They're professors.

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:39.852 size:81% position:41% line:92%
They're entrepreneurs.

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:40.987 size:88% position:44% line:92%
Refugee-owned businesses

00:03:40.987 --> 00:03:43.890 align:start size:94% position:6%
pay billions of dollars
in taxes every year

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.325 align:start size:94% position:6%
that goes
towards fire departments

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:47.560
and police departments
and schools and infrastructures,

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:51.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
all the things that we,
collectively, as Americans

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.532 size:84% position:42% line:92%
benefit from and enjoy.

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:56.869
And so, I, as a refugee
and a person who has resettled,

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:57.537 size:88% position:44% line:92%
actually happen to think

00:03:57.537 --> 00:04:01.174 align:start size:94% position:6%
that America is, in part,
better and stronger and richer

00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:04.143 align:start size:94% position:6%
because of refugees
and not despite them.

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:07.547 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:11.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Another thing it seems
like we always --

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:15.321 align:start size:94% position:6%
we focus on, you know,
what can we do for refugees?

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:16.356 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we focus a lot on --
on a number.

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:19.459
We should -- We should allow in
this many refugees a year

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:20.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
versus this many refugees
a year.

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:24.130
For those who actually get here,
and from your experience,

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.899 align:start size:94% position:6%
what is something
that you can do

00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:28.167 size:78% position:39%
if there are refugees
in your community?

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:32.138
What is a helpful thing that --
that refugees can use?

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:34.707 size:75% position:38%
-So much you can do,
even a small thing.

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:37.543 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, being a refugee
in a new country

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:39.646 align:start size:94% position:6%
is disorienting,
it's terribly confusing,

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:41.214 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I say that
from personal experience.

00:04:41.214 --> 00:04:44.884
So if there are refugees in your
community, reach out to them.

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:46.653 size:75% position:38%
Maybe they need help
with their English.

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:49.055 size:84% position:42%
Maybe they need help
with applying for a job

00:04:49.055 --> 00:04:50.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
or making
a doctor's appointment,

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:52.992 align:start size:94% position:6%
or applying for
a driver's license.

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:53.960 line:92%
You know, show them how to use

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:56.029 align:start size:94% position:6%
the public transportation
system.

00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:58.498
You know, if there
are refugee kids in your school,

00:04:58.498 --> 00:04:59.732 align:start size:94% position:6%
talk to your kids and say,
you know,

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:00.900 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Go up
and make friends with them.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
Ask questions.
Learn about their culture.

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:04.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know,
help them with homework.

00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:06.005 align:start size:94% position:6%
If they're being bullied,
stand up for them."

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:08.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, every little thing
that you do counts

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:11.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
and every small acts
of friendship and solidarity

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:13.379 line:92%
is appreciated and remembered.

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:15.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, thank you so much
for sharing that

00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:17.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
on your anniversary
of coming here.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:17.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Applause ]
-Thank you.

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:19.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We're certainly lucky
that you came,

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:20.953 size:78% position:39%
and thank you so much
for writing this book

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:21.621 size:88% position:44% line:92%
and all the work you do.

00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:23.556 align:start size:94% position:6%
I really appreciate it.
-Thank you.

